{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והבריח התיכון", :translit "veha-bariach ha-tichon", :gloss "and the middle bar"} {:he "הוא דוד", :translit "hu david", :gloss "he is David"} {:he "שמבריח הע\"א מן הקצה", :translit "she-mavriach ha-\"a min ha-katzeh", :gloss "who drives away the seventy-one from the end"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: בריח (bar) read as 'fugitive/escapee' (as in Amos 9:1–2), applied to David fleeing from his pursuers"}], :frame {:text "The Tanchuma identifies the middle bar of the Tabernacle with David, who embodies the enduring heart of Israel's kingdom (Tanchuma, Terumah 9). David is called a בריח in the sense of one who flees from danger — matching his years of flight from Saul and Absalom (1 Samuel 19–24; 2 Samuel 15–17). The number seventy-one evokes the Great Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 1:6), the supreme court of Israel; the Ba'al HaTurim compresses the idea that David's authority as king stands distinct from and above the seventy-one judges, so that he 'drives them away from the end', i.e., his royal governance is not subject to the Sanhedrin's final authority.", :sources ["Tanchuma Terumah 9" "Amos 9:1" "1 Samuel 19–24" "2 Samuel 15–17" "Sanhedrin 1:6"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: בריח (bar) read as 'fugitive/escapee' (as in Amos 9:1–2), applied to David fleeing from his pursuers", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:19:33.773983820Z", :model "glm-5.2"}